WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.640
Piacere
Girolamo

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:02.640
Trombetta.

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:03.969
What does that mean?

00:00:03.969 --> 00:00:08.670
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm revealing the secret meaning

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:13.150
of Alberto's catchphrase, and explaining the
many other hidden details and themes you might

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:15.190
have missed in the end of Luca.

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:17.550
Spoilers ahead so take care.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:22.380
The movie's bittersweet ending finds both
Luca and Alberto seizing the chance of a bright

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:26.990
new future, with Luca leaving for Genoa where
he'll pursue his passion for study, while

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:30.019
Alberto finds the father figure he longed
for in Massimo.

00:00:30.019 --> 00:00:34.280
And as the pair tearfully leave each other,
Alberto repeats the phrase he said to Luca

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:37.980
when they first met:
"Piacere, Girolamo Trombetta."

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:42.170
Back then, when Luca first asked him what
that meant, Alberto amusingly changed the

00:00:42.170 --> 00:00:44.489
subject;
but now at the end of the movie, he admits

00:00:44.489 --> 00:00:45.950
he never actually knew.

00:00:45.950 --> 00:00:47.560
"Go find out for me, will ya?"

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:52.059
Director Enrico Casarosa has since explained
that Alberto's catchphrase is a pun that comes

00:00:52.059 --> 00:00:56.930
from his own childhood, with the words "Piacere,
Girolamo Trombetta" translating roughly as

00:00:56.930 --> 00:01:00.219
"Nice to meet you, my name is twisty trombone".

00:01:00.219 --> 00:01:05.600
Notice how Alberto twists his hand and then
pulls and pushes Luca's arm during the handshake,

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.369
mimicking the twist in the name as well as
the slide of a trombone.

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:12.039
There's also a double meaning embedded in
this phrase.

00:01:12.039 --> 00:01:17.380
Firstly, the word "girolamo" is a play on
the Italian "giro la mano" which means "I

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:21.369
turn my hand", hence the "twisty" meaning
part of the pun.

00:01:21.369 --> 00:01:25.859
Secondly "Girolamo" is also the Italian equivalent
of the name "Jerome", which would make the

00:01:25.859 --> 00:01:30.240
literal translation of the phrase "Pleased
to meet you, my name is Jerome".

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:34.719
Like the "stupido" phrase, humour comes from
the fact that this is something Alberto overhead

00:01:34.719 --> 00:01:37.369
one day and didn't really understand what
it means.

00:01:37.369 --> 00:01:38.389
"It's a human thing.

00:01:38.389 --> 00:01:39.969
I'm kind of an expert."

00:01:39.969 --> 00:01:44.520
So although he kind of used it correctly when
he first met Luca, the second time he uses

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:49.259
it he's basically saying "pleased to meet
you" as they say goodbye, which makes their

00:01:49.259 --> 00:01:54.259
farewell even more touching in a way as he's
saying he's happy to know his friend.

00:01:54.259 --> 00:01:59.249
The double meanings of Alberto's parting phrase
nicely reflect the bigger theme of the characters'

00:01:59.249 --> 00:02:04.619
dual nature in the movie, which is highlighted
again in those final moments as the rain transforms

00:02:04.619 --> 00:02:09.220
Alberto back into his sea monster form, and
the same happens to Luca as the train pulls

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:14.390
out of the tunnel, with both of them no longer
afraid to show their true nature to the world.

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:18.920
That theme of difference and how individuals
may choose or feel forced to keep their real

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:23.790
selves a secret was also addressed in various
other moments from the end of the movie.

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:28.200
When Luca sees his friend Alberto cruelly
trapped in a net by Ercole after the rain

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:33.519
reveals his sea serpent nature, he decides
he won't pretend to be someone he isn't anymore

00:02:33.519 --> 00:02:37.760
as he did earlier when he shamed Alberto for
revealing himself as a sea monster.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:43.849
In a powerful moment, Luca sheds his colander
safety helmet and comes to his friend's rescue,

00:02:43.849 --> 00:02:46.599
revealing his own true colours at the same
time.

00:02:46.599 --> 00:02:52.080
On another level, Luca Paguro, whose surname
means "hermit crab" in Italian, is symbolically

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:56.260
casting off the protective shell he's worn
until now, something that hermit crabs do

00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:00.599
in real life as they grow and seek out a better
fitting shell or home.

00:03:00.599 --> 00:03:04.800
This scene is crucial for many reasons as
it has the power to speak to anyone who's

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:09.829
felt the need to hide who they really are
for fear of rejection, intolerance, and even

00:03:09.829 --> 00:03:14.390
violence from society,
which is one reason why Luca and Alberto's

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:19.069
transformations have been so readily seen
as a metaphor for the LGBTQ+ community.

00:03:19.069 --> 00:03:22.780
It's interesting then that "the feathery scaling"
effect of Mystique's transformation scenes

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:28.500
in the X-Men films, which are also allegories
for LGBTQ+ experiences, was one of the major

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:33.240
inspirations for the way Luca's animators
designed the transformation of the sea monsters

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:34.629
in this movie.

00:03:34.629 --> 00:03:39.859
Shortly after, we get another similar moment
when the two old ladies reveal a secret it

00:03:39.859 --> 00:03:44.700
feel likes they've been hiding for a very
long time, that they too are sea monsters.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:49.329
What these two old ladies represent is open
for the viewer to decide for themselves.

00:03:49.329 --> 00:03:53.340
On the one hand, their names Concetta and
Pinuccia Aragosta may suggest that they are

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:58.459
the aunts or some other relatives of the underwater
Aragosta family who Luca talks to at the beginning

00:03:58.459 --> 00:03:59.569
of the movie.

00:03:59.569 --> 00:04:01.780
"Hi, Mrs. Aragosta."

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:06.560
And you could also definitely view their sea
monster reveals as a metaphor for their coming

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:10.930
out, making them a lesbian couple who've been
living in secret in a society that they felt

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:12.739
would not openly accept them.

00:04:12.739 --> 00:04:18.299
Given the 50s to 60s period that Luca is set
in, the reality of gay couples living together

00:04:18.299 --> 00:04:22.330
claiming to be relatives or just platonic
friends was not uncommon.

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:26.590
Check out the touching Netflix documentary
'Secret Love', for example.

00:04:26.590 --> 00:04:31.430
Also notice how the two ladies in Luca live
on the outskirts of town near the Old Tower,

00:04:31.430 --> 00:04:36.530
a nod towards their feeling of being outsiders
and needing somewhere they could be safe.

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:41.660
Casarosa specifically addressed these metaphors
in an interview with SlashFilm where he explained

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:45.889
that he loved the idea that the film explores
the feeling of otherness and difference and

00:04:45.889 --> 00:04:49.479
he added that the story is about a person
making the decision to say

00:04:49.479 --> 00:04:50.569
"Here I am.

00:04:50.569 --> 00:04:52.240
Let the chips fall where they may.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:54.990
I'm going to fly my flag and I don't care."

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:59.860
The film also acknowledges that other people's
acceptance of who you are isn't necessarily

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:04.830
something that will either come easily or
sometimes even at all, but what's important

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:07.910
is finding your own tribe so that you can
thrive.

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:10.020
"Some people, they'll never accept him.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:11.020
But some will.

00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:14.139
And he seems to know how to find the good
ones."

00:05:14.139 --> 00:05:18.450
As Casarosa says, good friends are important
"because they are the people that see you

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:22.130
for who you are, and they don't care about
whatever differences you have."

00:05:22.130 --> 00:05:25.580
And you can see this in how Luca and Giulia
support each other's passions.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:29.560
"Just promise you'll tell me everything you
see!

00:05:29.560 --> 00:05:30.560
Sorry.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:31.560
Too much?"

00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:32.560
"Never."

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:33.560
"Sorry.

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:34.560
Too much?"

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:35.560
"No way."

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:38.690
We also see this theme of outsiders and the
feeling of being rejected or feared by society

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:40.440
in several other characters.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:45.760
Ercole, the villain of this story, calls Luca
and Alberto "out-of-towners" before proceeding

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:49.930
to mock the way they dress and use his goons
to bully the pair.

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:54.190
Giulia lives with her mother in Genoa for
most of the year and doesn't feel fully accepted

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:57.600
by the community in Portorosso when she comes
to visit her father.

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:03.400
"Every summer, I come here, and everyone thinks
I'm just some weird kid who doesn't belong.

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:06.410
Which is one reason why she may feel especially
drawn to Luca and Alberto.

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:07.610
"You know, we underdogs have to look out for
each other, right?"

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:12.690
Another reason being her strong sense of justice
against society's intolerance as represented

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:13.690
by Ercole.

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:14.690
"So long, evil empire of injustice."

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:20.180
And when Massimo refers to his disability,
pointing out that he was born that way and

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:24.560
that his right arm wasn't bitten off by a
sea monster, the film is making the point

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:28.570
that we shouldn't make assumptions about people
based on their appearances.

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:33.580
Showing disability as something that simply
exists and is just one part of who someone

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:37.669
is is all-too-rare on screen, so it's good
to see it here.

00:06:37.669 --> 00:06:42.580
Massimo himself shows how a person can overcome
their fears of what they think of as "the

00:06:42.580 --> 00:06:47.659
other", as he progresses from being obsessed
with hunting sea monsters to adopting Alberto

00:06:47.659 --> 00:06:51.830
and even visiting Luca's sea monster family
during the end credits.

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:55.870
Even Ercole's sidekicks decide they've had
enough of being abused by him at the end of

00:06:55.870 --> 00:07:00.841
the film and reject him and what he represents
by tossing him into the town fountain, which

00:07:00.841 --> 00:07:04.349
is what he tried to do to Luca when he first
met him.

00:07:04.349 --> 00:07:08.659
Casarosa says that he loves the idea that
the film's sea monsters are a way for "the

00:07:08.659 --> 00:07:12.699
audience to come in and bring their own experience
of feeling 'other'" and project their own

00:07:12.699 --> 00:07:14.430
sense of difference onto it.

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:17.800
Now, I want to mention a few other details
you might have missed from the end of the

00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:18.800
movie.

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:22.979
As the credits roll, the accompanying images
reveal the story of what happened after Luca

00:07:22.979 --> 00:07:24.350
left Portorosso.

00:07:24.350 --> 00:07:28.720
We can see that Massimo has fully adopted
Alberto as his son, and when he gives him

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:32.689
a knife for a birthday present, it pays off
this earlier scene in the movie.

00:07:32.689 --> 00:07:33.900
"Which knife do I get?

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:34.900
Huh?

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:35.900
Huh?"

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:36.900
"You don't."

00:07:36.900 --> 00:07:40.240
And as well as helping out Massimo, Alberto
also now seems to have a job as a lifeguard.

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:45.199
Also, the statue of the town's famed sea monster
killer appears to have been replaced by Luca's

00:07:45.199 --> 00:07:49.910
stone statue of himself that he used as a
decoy for his illicit trips to the surface,

00:07:49.910 --> 00:07:53.629
which shows just how much attitudes have changed
in Portorosso.

00:07:53.629 --> 00:07:58.050
As for Luca and Giulia, they get a dog together
called "Nerone", the Italian name for the

00:07:58.050 --> 00:07:59.650
Roman Emperor Nero.

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:03.719
The turtle from the island that held up the
ramp for Alberto and Luca's homemade Vespa

00:08:03.719 --> 00:08:06.990
jump has been adopted too and given the name
Caligula.

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:11.081
And many thanks to Pier Gio who commented
on my easter eggs video that "Caligula was

00:08:11.081 --> 00:08:15.729
a mad Roman emperor, who named his horse a
senator and ordered a war against the sea

00:08:15.729 --> 00:08:16.909
god Neptune."

00:08:16.909 --> 00:08:21.449
Machiavelli has a litter of kittens, many
of which are mini-moustachioed versions of

00:08:21.449 --> 00:08:25.550
him, and his name is a reference to the Italian
writer and political philosopher whose most

00:08:25.550 --> 00:08:28.310
famous work The Prince has a notable quote
that:

00:08:28.310 --> 00:08:32.360
"Men usually judge things by the eye rather
than by the hand.

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:36.020
Everyone sees what you appear to be, but few
feel what you are."

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:39.420
Which feels especially pertinent to the story
of Luca.

00:08:39.420 --> 00:08:43.600
Some other little details you might have missed
in the end credits include Luca and Giulia

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:48.150
indulging their joy of space as they watch
the moon landing, which as Charles Lee points

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:50.170
out was in 1969.

00:08:50.170 --> 00:08:54.920
There's also a Lady and the Tramp easter egg
with two cats sharing a plate of spaghetti.

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:58.730
And the cheese, basil, garlic, and nuts that
appear at the end of the credits are most

00:08:58.730 --> 00:09:03.520
of the ingredients needed to make classic
pesto, an essential part of Giulia's favourite

00:09:03.520 --> 00:09:04.520
pasta dish.

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:07.120
"I made your favourite, trenette al pesto."

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:11.420
The post-credits scene also reveals what might
have been in store for Luca if he'd ended

00:09:11.420 --> 00:09:16.260
up going to the deep with his Uncle Ugo as
the strange fish chatters on about how great

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:18.210
life is at the bottom of the sea.

00:09:18.210 --> 00:09:19.210
"Mmm.

00:09:19.210 --> 00:09:20.210
Whale carcass.

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:21.730
Anyway, you're gonna have fun."

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:26.830
The visual punchline at the end though is
that all this time Ugo's been talking to a

00:09:26.830 --> 00:09:32.470
solitary goatfish who sidles off once he realises
exactly what life's going to be like with

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:33.600
Ugo in the deep.

00:09:33.600 --> 00:09:39.220
A fun theory I had, and which Cepterman also
noticed, is that this goatfish could be Enrico,

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:41.230
who Luca mentioned at the beginning of the
movie.

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:46.020
"You want to run off like your buddy Enrico?

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:48.850
He's either dead, or he's out there.

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:49.850
Somewhere."

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:53.730
I also love their idea that that line could
be a Finding Nemo reference too.

00:09:53.730 --> 00:09:58.600
On top of that, this goatfish Enrico is quite
likely also a reference to director Enrico

00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:03.680
Casarosa himself, who grew up in Italy, but
left his home to go to New York and pursue

00:10:03.680 --> 00:10:08.220
his passion for animation, eventually getting
to work and direct films at Pixar.

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:13.360
In fact, Casarosa has said that Luca's story
very much channels his own childhood and how,

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:18.560
as a shy kid of 11, he met his real-life best
friend Alberto, who was a much more boisterous

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:21.230
character, just like the one in the movie.

00:10:21.230 --> 00:10:26.650
Alternately, the goatfish with Uncle Ugo might
be Giuseppe who had a habit of drifting off

00:10:26.650 --> 00:10:27.650
by himself.

00:10:27.650 --> 00:10:29.600
"Giuseppe, get back here!"

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:34.090
And thanks to "Just Someone" who pointed out
the scene may also be a nod to Finding Nemo's

00:10:34.090 --> 00:10:38.810
post-credits scene in which an anglerfish
with a glowing lure like Uncle Ugo's gets

00:10:38.810 --> 00:10:41.330
eaten up by tiny little green fish.

00:10:41.330 --> 00:10:45.980
Now, as great as the ending of Luca was, the
original story was going to be incredibly

00:10:45.980 --> 00:10:50.540
different with a big battle and Alberto turning
into a giant kraken.

00:10:50.540 --> 00:10:54.700
I'll be explaining all that in my next Luca
video, and I'll add a link to watch it here

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:57.410
and in the video description as soon as it's
ready.

00:10:57.410 --> 00:11:02.070
So what did you think of Luca, Alberto and
Giulia's story and what does the movie mean

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:03.070
for you?

00:11:03.070 --> 00:11:04.730
Leave your thoughts in the comments below!

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:09.211
And if you enjoyed this video, then don't
forget to hit that thumbs-up and share, it's

00:11:09.211 --> 00:11:10.830
hugely appreciated.

